WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.400
87% of Americans think it's appropriate
to require physically able people to

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.849
seek work in order to receive public
benefits, and generally most government

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:19.320
aid recipients do. Yet, critics call work
requirements for safety net programs

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.359
"cruel", believing they punish people for
being poor. The truth is, work

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:28.470
requirements are not punishment. They
ensure that people who are capable of

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.309
work stay connected to the labor market.
Most states do this with people

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:35.969
receiving unemployment compensation, and
they should be doing it with safety net

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:40.680
programs, too. Requiring work sends a
clear message that if you can work, you

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.280
should be working, while government
assistance is there to support you. It

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.539
also fulfills a kind of social contract
that most poor people agree with. In a

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:53.129
poll conducted in 2016,
eight of ten Americans in poverty agreed

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:57.270
with work requirements. Most of all, work
requirements improve the integrity of

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:01.199
our safety net programs. Anti-poverty
programs do much better when they are

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:05.040
perceived as a way to help people move
out of poverty through work rather than

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.040
simply a means to make unemployment more
tolerable.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.440
What do you think about

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:13.320
requiring people to work in exchange for
public benefits? Let us know in your

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.369
comments. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:21.600
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.229
from AEI.

